The picker is currently hard-coded to expose only two capability filters:

[ModelSelector.tsx](/home/diogenes/Projects/keating/web/src/components/ModelSelector.tsx:35)

```ts
const CAPABILITY_FILTERS = [
  { value: "all", label: "All capabilities" },
  { value: "thinking", label: "Thinking" },
  { value: "vision", label: "Vision" },
];
```

Add another filter and define how to detect it:

```ts
const CAPABILITY_FILTERS = [
  { value: "all", label: "All capabilities" },
  { value: "thinking", label: "Thinking" },
  { value: "vision", label: "Vision" },
  { value: "text", label: "Text" },
  { value: "audio", label: "Audio" },
] as const;

function modelHasCapability(model: Model<Api>, capability: CapabilityFilter): boolean {
  switch (capability) {
    case "thinking":
      return model.reasoning;
    case "vision":
      return model.input.includes("image");
    case "text":
      return model.input.includes("text");
    case "audio":
      return model.input.includes("audio");
    default:
      return true;
  }
}
```

You would also update the type if needed:

```ts
type CapabilityFilter = (typeof CAPABILITY_FILTERS)[number]["value"];
```

That type already derives automatically from the filter list.

The important limitation is that the model metadata currently only reliably exposes `reasoning` and `input` modalities. Custom models are also stored with only `reasoning` and `vision` flags in [model-prefs.ts](/home/diogenes/Projects/keating/web/src/keating/model-prefs.ts:7). For capabilities such as tool calling, structured output, image generation, audio output, or embeddings, you’ll need to add those fields to `SavedModel`, preserve them in `buildSavedModel()`, and ensure provider discovery returns equivalent metadata.

